# Amorphization Engineering of Bimetallic Metal‐Organic Frameworks to Identify Volcano‐Type Trend toward Oxygen Evolution Reaction

## abstract
Amorphous metal‐organic frameworks (MOFs) with aperiodic atomic arrangements, featuring high intrinsic activity and rich active sites, have emerged as promising oxygen evolution reaction (OER) catalysts. However, the quantitative structure‐activity relationships (SARs) that determine the OER activity, the key to a rational catalyst design, remain unresolved. Inspired by controllable amorphization engineering, the amorphous MOF structures are rationally constructed as an ideal platform to explore the SAR in catalyzing OER. The mechanistic studies show that the OER activity could be volcano‐shape correlated with either constant adsorption energy difference between OOH* and O* (ΔGOOH* − ΔGO*) or the position of the d band center. The amorphous MOF (Ni8Co2‐BDC), situated close to the volcano summit, possesses an appropriate Ed energy level, which exhibits the balanced intermediates adsorption/desorption ability and consequently results in the boosted catalytic activity and long‐term stability. This work supplies new perspectives to investigate the SAR in amorphous MOF structures, thereby guiding the rational design of advanced OER catalysts.

## 1. Introduction
The overall water splitting (OWS), producing high-purity hydrogen driven by renewable energy, has been widely recognized as the fascinating solution to address the impending global energy demand and environmental deterioration.[1] One of its core processes is closely associated with the anodic oxygen evolution reaction (OER), involving the thermodynamically uphill reaction with sluggish proton-coupled electron transfer kinetics, which in turn severely hampers the OWS efficiency.[1b,2] Currently, precious noble metal Ir/Ru-based materials are still first-rate OER catalysts, nevertheless, their scarcity and high-cost limit the actual application.[3] Tremendous advances have been intensively investigated in designing earth-abundant and cost-effective OER catalysts with high catalytic activity and long-term stability.[4] Particularly, the development of a series of first-row transition metal oxides,[4a] (oxy)hydroxides,[4c] carbides,[4d] phosphides,[4e] and sulfides[4g] demonstrate their significant potential as viable alternatives in catalyzing the OER. Current electrocatalysts, despite extensive efforts, have not yet satisfied the stringent demands of industrial applications. In this regard, developing rational strategies to realize the construction of efficient catalysts toward OER is highly attractive but continues to pose a significant challenge to date.
## 2. Results and Discussion
As schematically illustrated in Figure 1a, an interface-mediated strategy is utilized to synthesize a series of bimetallic $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{\gamma}.$ BDC MOFs by tailoring the desired MOF growth dynamics.[10] Three solvent layers, miscible $N_{:}$ $N$ -dimethylformamide, and acetonitrile $\mathrm{(DMF/CH_{3}C N)}$ with varied ratios, are vertically arranged under their different densities. The feeding $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{Co}$ metal salts at the top solvent layer and terephthalic acid $\ensuremath{\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\right)}$ at the bottom solvent layer could meet at the intermediate region through diffusion modulation. The six $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{\gamma}$ -BDC MOFs are finally constructed by tailoring the appropriate ratios of $\mathrm{Ni/Co}$ metals (Figure S1, Supporting Information), which are denoted as Ni-BDC, $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC, $\mathrm{Ni}_{6}\mathrm{Co}_{4}$ -BDC, $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{Co}_{6}$ -BDC, $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{8}$ - BDC, and Co-BDC, respectively. Additionally, inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES) is performed to investigate the content of the element, where the ratio of $\mathrm{Ni/Co)}$ 1 is similar to that of the starting ratio, suggesting the successful formation of MOF structure (Table S1, Supporting Information).
The morphology of the as-fabricated Ni-BDC is characterized by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) in Figure S2, Supporting Information, where the hierarchical foam-like nanostructures can be observed. No significant lattice fringes could be captured from the high-resolution TEM (HRTEM), suggesting the amorphous state (Figure 1b,c).[5c,13] As illustrated in Figures S3 and S4, Supporting Information, the Co-BDC shows the typical 2D nanosheet structure, exhibiting a uniform thickness of about $4\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which is consistent with five metal coordination layers or four coordination structural layers (Figure S5, Supporting Information).[3b] The lattice spacing of $1.05\mathrm{nm}$ matched well with the (200) plane of Co-BDC can be obviously viewed in the HRTEM image (Figure 1e,f), reflecting the typical characteristics of high crystallinity (Figure S6, Supporting Information).[11] For the $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC (Figure S7, Supporting Information), the hierarchical foam-like structure is basically maintained for comparison with that of Ni-BDC. Similarly, no obvious lattice fringes can be captured from the HRTEM image (Figure 1h), implying the amorphous feature (Figure 1i). The energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) elements mapping illustrates that C, O, Ni, and Co are uniformly spread throughout the whole structure (Figure 3d,g,j, and Figure S8, Supporting Information).
To further identify the successful formation and structure evolution of six $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{\gamma}$ -BDC MOFs, X-ray diffraction (XRD) is performed (Figure 2a). The XRD pattern of the Co-BDC is similar to that of the simulated $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{8}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{4})$ structure (Figure S9, Supporting Information), where the diffraction peaks at ${\approx}8.8^{\circ}$ , $15.5^{\circ}$ , $17.7^{\circ}$ , and $27.9^{\circ}$ assigned to (200), (001), (202), and $(-201)$ facets indicate its well-crystalline structure.[3b] Interestingly, the main diffraction peaks become weaker along with the Ni incorporation into Co-BDC, evidencing their gradually decreased crystallinity. When the $\mathrm{Ni/Co}$ feeding ratio is $8/2$ for $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ - BDC and $10/0$ for Ni-BDC, only a broad “hump” at ${\approx}8.9^{\circ}$ can be captured, implying the distinct short-range atomic ordering but long-range disordered structure.[5c] The electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectroscopy reveals the presence of oxygen vacancy in $\mathrm{Ni}_{6}\mathrm{Co}_{4}$ -BDC, $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{Co}_{6}$ -BDC, $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{8}$ -BDC, Co-BDC, and Ni-BDC, as evidenced by the asymmetric peak observed around $g=2.002$ .[14] In addition, the $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC exhibits a pair of sharper peaks at around $g=2.003$ compared with other MOFbased catalysts (Figure S10, Supporting Information), indicating more oxygen vacancy generated within the amorphous structure, which is favorable to expose more unsaturated active sites on the surface.[15] Density functional theory (DFT) calculations are further performed to gain an in-depth understanding of such crystalline structure evolution, in which the defects formation energy $(E_{\mathrm{Formation}})$ is calculated for four typical simulated models including Co sites in Co-BDC, Ni sites in Ni-BDC, Ni sites in $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC and Co sites in $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC (Figure 2b and Figure S11, Supporting Information). The $E_{\mathrm{formation}}$ for generating unsaturated coordinated Co sites in pure Co-BDC is calculated to be $+0.22\mathrm{eV}$ indicating the formation of defective Co-BDC might follow the feature of the endothermic pathway, which proves that pure Co-BDC is more stable compared with the defective one. In contrast, the $E_{\mathrm{Formation}}$ for pure Ni-BDC possesses the negative value of $-0.69\ \mathrm{eV},$ suggesting the character of the exothermic reaction, which helps to generate more unsaturated coordinated Ni sites within the MOF structure and thus facilitates the formation of the local disordered structure.[11] As seen, the $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}C o_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{BDC}$ also shows the negative $E_{\mathrm{Formation}}$ value for generating unsaturated coordinated Ni sites $(-1.48\ \mathrm{eV})$ and Co sites $(-1.32~\mathrm{eV})$ , indicating the similar feature of exothermic reaction as the defective Ni-BDC. On the basis of the above XRD, EPR, and theoretical calculation results, it can be clearly seen that the amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC with the short-range ordered structure could be readily constructed by tailoring the ratio of feeding $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{Co}$ . Such disordered structure reflects more active sites exposed within the catalysts, promotes the redistribution of the local electrons, and thus possesses the great potential to realize the enhanced OER activity.
Raman and Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra are then recorded to investigate the functional groups of the MOF structure.[16] As shown in Figure 2c, the vibrational bands captured at ${\approx}632$ and $860~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ could be assigned to the $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ stretching region of the benzene ring.[17] The adsorption of in and out-of-phase stretching regions is also visible for the carboxylate group at ${\approx}1431$ and $1609~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ .[5c] Figure 2d shows that the amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC exhibits the band at ${\approx}3440$ and $750~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ resulting from the $_\mathrm{O-H}$ stretching mode and $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}$ bonding vibrations of the benzene rings, respectively. Meanwhile, the terephthalic acid exhibits a more complex set of vibrational fingerprints, encompassing both in-plane (1612, 1450, 1180, and $1130{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}.$ ) and out-of-plane (832, 805, and 630 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ ) deformation modes.[18] The peaks at ${\approx}1575$ and 1386 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be ascribed to the $\nu_{\mathrm{asym}}$ $(-\mathsf{C O O^{-}})$ and $\nu_{\mathrm{sym}}$ $(-\mathsf{C O O^{-}})$ of the organic linker that coordinated with the metal centers, implying exposed carboxylate groups anchored on the surface of the MOF structure.[19] This would be helpful to accelerate the adsorption of water in catalyzing OER, which is further evidenced by the gradually decreased contact angle from $108.41^{\circ}$ (pure carbon cloth), $98.23^{\circ}$ (Co-BDC), $68.39^{\circ}$ (Ni-BDC), to $58.15^{\circ}$ $(\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC), as shown in Figure 2e and Figure S12, Supporting Information. The existing peak at $539~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ could be attributed to the vibration of coordination bonds between the metal atoms $(\mathrm{Ni}/{\mathrm{Co}})$ and the carboxylic groups of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ , manifesting the metal ions are successfully coordinated with the $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ ligand.
X-ray absorption structure (XAS) analysis is performed to further elucidate the chemical state and local coordination environment of amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC at the atomic level, together with the Co foil, Ni foil, CoO, ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ , NiO, Co-BDC, and Ni-BDC as the comparison. The Co K-edge X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectra exhibit that Co-BDC and $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC possess a similar profile as CoO (Figure 4a), implying the identical Co coordination structure.[12] The first derivative XANES data is extracted to gain information on the $E_{0}$ values (first inflection point on the absorption edge), as shown in the inset in Figure 4a. The Co-BDC and $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC show the fitted $E_{0}$ situated at 7719.4 and $7719.3\mathrm{eV},$ respectively, which are similar to that of CoO (7720.7 eV) but much larger than that of Co foil $(7709\mathrm{eV})$ , verifying average valence state of Co is $+2$ .[5c] As for Ni K-edge, the near-edge peaks of Ni-BDC and $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC are similar to that of NiO reference (Figure 4b), signifying the cationic environment of Ni. As shown in the inset in Figure 4b, the first derivative XANES spectra at the Ni K-edge exhibit that Ni-BDC and $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC own the fitted $E_{0}$ values of 8344.4 and $8345\mathrm{eV_{\mathrm{:}}}$ respectively, which are close to that of NiO $(8345.2\ \mathrm{eV})$ , demonstrating the presence of $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ .[26] To further unravel the efficient coupling effect between Co and Ni metal centers at the atomic level, their detailed electron numbers are calculated by integrating the projected density of states (DOS) (Figure 4c,d). Figure 4e shows that the electron numbers in $3\mathrm{d}\mathrm{Co}$ orbital are 7.06 and 7.19 for Co-BDC and $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC, respectively, indicating the reduced valence state of the Co atom in $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC. The $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ - BDC exhibits the electron number of 8.11 in 3d Ni orbital, lower than 8.12 for that in Ni-BDC, verifying its increased valence state of Ni atom.[17] Consequently, such coupling is expected to trigger the efficient electron transfer from Ni to Co atoms through the ${\mathsf{O}}^{2-}$ -bridging, thereby further optimizing the OER activity.[27]
The Fourier transform (FT) $\mathrm{k}^{3}$ -weighted extended X-ray absorption fine structure (FT-EXAFS) spectra are shown in Figure 4f. It can be observed that a prominent peak located at about $1.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ is associated with the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-O}$ bond at the first shell for both Co-BDC and $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC.[3a] The other relatively weak peaks are attributed to $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-C}$ (2.36 Å), Co─O (2.86 Å), and Co─O─Co $(3.25\mathrm{\AA})$ bonds in the higher shells, respectively. The Ni-BDC and $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC have a first shell Ni─O peak at 1.56 Å, and the relatively weak Ni─C (2.36 Å), Ni─O (2.86 Å), and Ni─O─Ni (3.25 Å) peaks in the higher shells (Figure 4g). The coordination configuration of Co-BDC, $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC, and Ni-BDC is further analyzed by theoretical calculations (Figures S15–S17, Supporting Information). The coordination number of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-O}$ is calculated as 6.1 in the first coordination shell of Co-BDC, proving the building blocks are ${\mathsf{C o O}}_{6}$ octahedra (Table S2, Supporting Information). In contrast, the amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC possesses a lower coordination number for $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-O}$ (5.7) and Ni─O (5.4), implying the existence of coordinatively unsaturated ${\mathsf{C o O}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{NiO}_{6}$ sites, which is consistent well with the reduced $E_{\mathrm{Formation}}$ results (Figure 2b). EXAFS wavelet transforms (WT) analysis (Figure S18, Supporting Information), providing detailed information on R- and k-space is also implemented. Figure 4h shows the intensity maximum at ${\approx}3.8\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}$ for the amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ - BDC, well matching with the location of the ${\mathrm{Co}}/{\mathrm{Ni}}{\mathrm{-O}}$ path, which are also observed in the Co-BDC and Ni-BDC. The prominent intensity signal associated with Co─Co and Ni─Ni bond is captured at ${\approx}7.4\mathring\mathrm{A}^{-1}$ for Co and Ni foil, but could not be detected for the amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC, implying the only atomically dispersed Ni and Co sites within the MOF structure. The above-mentioned theoretical and experimental analysis clearly demonstrates that the amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC possesses coordinatively unsaturated $\mathrm{Ni/Co}$ sites, which are expected to promote OER activity.
Encouraged by the unique hierarchical foam-like morphology, optimized electronic structures, and abundant coordinatively unsaturated sites, the electrochemical OER activities of assynthesized $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{\gamma}$ -BDC are first evaluated in $1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{KOH}$ aqueous solution. For comparison, commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ is also tested as the benchmark catalyst under the same conditions. As shown in Figure 5a, the amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC exhibits an impressive overpotential $(\eta)$ of $274~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\ \mathrm{mA\cm^{-2}}$ obtained from the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves, which is much better than those of $\mathrm{Ni}_{6}\mathrm{Co}_{4}$ -BDC $(299\mathrm{mV})$ , $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{Co}_{6}$ -BDC $(309\mathrm{mV})$ ), $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{8}$ -BDC $\{330~\mathrm{mV}\}$ ), Ni-BDC ( $\{324~\mathrm{{mV}}\}$ ), Co-BDC $(353~\mathrm{mV})$ , and even surpass the state-of-the-art $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ $(336~\mathrm{mV})$ . The superior OER activity can also be evidenced by the low overpotential achieved at high current densities of 50 and $100\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ (Figure S19, Supporting Information). It is worth noting that the carbon cloth is non-active to the OER process, which functions primarily as a carrier for the MOF-based catalyst, without exerting a substantial influence on the intrinsic activity of the catalyst. The corresponding Tafel plots are also calculated from the polarization curves to shed more insight into the electrochemical reaction kinetics.[28] As shown in Figure 5b, the amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ - BDC possesses the lowest Tafel slope of $73.1~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ , better than those of $\mathrm{Ni}_{6}\mathrm{Co}_{4}$ -BDC $(75.4\:\mathrm{mV\:dec^{-1}},$ ), $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{Co}_{6}$ -BDC $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{82\mathrm{mV}}}$ $\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}$ ), $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{8}$ -BDC $(76.5\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ ), Ni-BDC $(102.1\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ ), Co-BDC $(75.8\:\mathrm{mV}\:\mathrm{dec^{-1}})$ ), and also commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ $115.4\mathrm{mV}$ $\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}$ ), implying the faster charge transfer and accelerated OER kinetics.[29] The mass transfer capacity of $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC decreases significantly at high current densities, even lower than that of $\mathrm{Ni}_{6}\mathrm{Co}_{4}$ -BDC and $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{Co}_{6}$ -BDC, as evidenced by the decreased linear slope from Figure 5a. This phenomenon possibly originates from the significantly reduced crystallinity of amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ - BDC, which increases its solubility in alkaline aqueous solution, leading to its decreased mass transfer capacity at high current densities under harsh conditions.[30] In terms of the overpotential and Tafel slope, the amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC is comparable or even superior to most typical reported OER catalysts (Figure S20 and Table S3, Supporting Information). These above results suggest the OER activity forms the volcano-typed correlation with the $\mathrm{Ni/Co}$ ratio, where the amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC is located close to the volcanic peak with the highest OER activity (Figure 5c and Figure S21, Supporting Information).
Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) is then implemented to further evaluate the charge transfer kinetics. Figure 5d presents that the amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC depicts the smallest semicircle diameter compared with the other counterparts, implying its lowest charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ occurring at the electrode/electrolyte interface, which is helpful to promote the OER activity via accelerating the electron transfer. The optimized charge transfer capability may be derived from the amorphous structures and coupling between Ni and Co ions, which could be advantageous to accelerate the transport of interfacial charged species.[31] To unveil the origin of the intrinsic catalytic activity of the amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC, the double-layer capacitances $(C_{\mathrm{dl}})$ are also calculated to assess the electrochemical active surface areas (ECSA) via cyclic voltammograms (CV, Figure S22, Supporting Information) in the non-Faradaic potential region.
Apparently, an improved $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ representing more exposed active sites can be captured in Figure 5e, among which the $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC possesses the highest value of $4.11\mathrm{~mF~cm}^{-2}$ , in line with its highest OER activity. We also evaluate the intrinsic OER activities of as-synthesized $\mathrm{Ni}_{\boldsymbol{x}}\mathrm{Co}_{\boldsymbol{\gamma}}$ -BDC samples in terms of turnover frequency (TOF) and mass activity (MA) with the potential fixed at 1.53 V (Figures S23 and S24, Supporting Information). Significantly, the amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC exhibits the highest TOF of $17.85~\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ and MA of $23.61\mathrm{Ag^{-1}}$ among all the tested catalysts, which implies its excellent intrinsic catalytic activity. Furthermore, the overall water-splitting activity is evaluated using $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ and amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC electrodes as both the cathode and anode materials. The polarization curves for OWS depicted in Figure S25a, Supporting Information, reveal that a cell voltage of only $1.52\mathrm{V}$ is achieved at $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ for $\mathrm{Pt/C||Ni_{8}C o_{2}\cdot H}$ DC, while a higher voltage of $1.55\mathrm{~V~}$ is required for commercial $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}||\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ . Additionally, the Faradaic efficiencies, requiring from $\mathrm{Pt/C||Ni_{8}C o_{2}}$ -BDC electrode, are measured at the current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ during OWS. As shown in Figure S25b, Supporting Information, the stable hydrogen/oxygen yield rates can be observed from our $\mathrm{Pt/C||Ni_{8}C o_{2}}$ -BDC electrode, in line with the theoretical values, which implies the high selectivity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ during the water splitting.
As the long-term durability of catalysts is an important criterion to evaluate their practicality, the chronopotentiometry test is implemented (Figure S26, Supporting Information). It can be observed that the amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC exhibits negligible potential change for nearly $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ at the current density of $10~\mathrm{mA}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ compared with the benchmark $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ , verifying the extraordinary long-time stability. Considering the actual application scenarios, we conduct additional evaluations to assess the long-term stability under the high current density of 50 and $100\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , discovering that the amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC could steadily operate for a long period (Figure S27, Supporting Information). To further evaluate the stability under a wide current density window, the multistep-chronopotentiometry curves from 10 to $100\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ are recorded (Figure S28, Supporting Information). As observed, no discernible alterations can be captured in the applied potential of each stage. Considering the heightened practical importance of the typical OER process at high current densities, further enhancing the stability of the synthesized MOF-based catalysts becomes imperative. This can be achieved through the potential strategies proposed below, including the in situ growth of MOFs on conductive substrates or the adjustment of its morphology and electronic structure to enable effective adsorption/desorption of oxygen-containing intermediates in catalyzing OER at high current densities. Additionally, it is feasible to employ azo-based ligands and low-valent metal ions according to the principles of the hard-soft-acid-base (HSAB) theory or to use carboxylate-based ligands and high-valent metal ions to enhance the metal-oxygen bond strength during the construction of highly stable MOF-based catalysts.[32] Besides, the XPS, SEM, and TEM are conducted to evaluate structure stability for amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC after the 48-h durability test. As seen from the XPS survey spectra before and after the OER test, no distinct changes can be observed, except for the appearance of an additional F 1s signal originating from Nafion (Figure S29, Supporting Information). Two new peaks derived from the $\mathrm{Ni^{3+}}$ with the binding energy of 875.6 and $857.6\mathrm{eV}$ can be observed from the $\mathsf{N i}2\mathsf{p}$ high-resolution XPS spectrum, indicating partial $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ to form high-valence $\mathrm{Ni^{3+}}$ in catalyzing OER (Figure S30a, Supporting Information).[33] For the Co 2p high-resolution XPS spectra, the existence of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ is confirmed by the peaks at ${\approx}781.4\$ and $797.5\ \mathrm{eV},$ which implies partial ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ oxidized to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ after the $48\mathrm{h}$ durability test (Figure S30b, Supporting Information).[34] As shown in Figure S31, Supporting Information, the morphology of amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC exhibits no noticeable changes after undergoing the OER test. The HRTEM image still displays the ambiguous lattice fringes as before, suggesting the amorphous structure remains maintained after OER. Furthermore, the corresponding EDX element mapping presents the uniform distribution of C, O, Co, and Ni, evidencing excellent structural stability.
