To further clarify the amorphous nature that significantly influences $E_{\mathrm{d}}$ energy level and OER activities in our system, the corresponding highly crystalline $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{\gamma}$ -BDC (denoted as $\mathbf{c}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{\gamma}$ -BDC) is synthesized using the traditional hydrothermal method.[25] The successful formation of the MOF structure and the feature of high crystallinity are confirmed by the XRD patterns (Figure S34, Supporting Information). The VBS of c$\mathrm{Ni}_{_{x}}\mathrm{Co}_{\gamma}$ -BDC is then recorded to investigate the changes in the $E_{\mathrm{d}}$ energy level (Figure S35, Supporting Information).[8b,40] As observed, the position of the d-band center gradually shifts upward from c-Ni-BDC to $\mathsf{c}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{N i}_{6}\mathsf{C o}_{4}$ -BDC and then drops down from $\mathrm{\c{-}N i_{6}C o_{4}}$ -BDC to $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{8}$ -BDC, except for the largest deviation observed in Co-BDC. Such changes highlight the sensitivity of the d-band center to variations in the $\mathrm{Ni/Co}$ ratio within the c$\mathrm{Ni}_{\boldsymbol{x}}\mathrm{Co}_{\boldsymbol{\gamma}}$ -BDC catalysts. In addition, the $\mathrm{\c{-}N i_{6}C o_{4}}$ -BDC possesses excellent OER activity with an overpotential of $286\mathrm{mV}$ at the current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , especially better than the $\mathsf{c}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{N i}_{8}\mathsf{C o}_{2}$ - BDC $(298~\mathrm{mV},$ Figure S36a, Supporting Information). This interesting phenomenon suggests that the optimal catalytic performance in the highly crystalline MOF-based system might arise from the bimetallic synergy when the $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{Co}$ ratio is $6/4$ , rather than 8/2. Compared with $\mathrm{c}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ni}_{6}\mathrm{Co}_{4}$ -BDC, the $E_{\mathrm{d}}$ energy level of $\mathsf{c}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{N i}_{8}\mathsf{C o}_{2}$ is much closer to $E_{\mathrm{fremi}}$ $(E_{\mathrm{f}})$ , making the adsorption of oxygen-related species stronger, which in turn hamper the desorption process of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ in catalyzing OER, and thus leading to the limitation in further improving the catalytic activity. That is why the $\mathrm{\c{-}N i_{8}C o_{2}}$ -BDC is situated at the waist of the volcano plot recording the relationship between the catalytic activity and the $E_{\mathrm{d}}$ energy level (Figure S36b, Supporting Information). However, a significant increase in catalytic activity is observed when the $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{Co}$ ratio is $^{8/2}$ along with the amorphous nature in amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC, which is even better than the $\mathsf{c}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{N i}_{6}\mathsf{C o}_{4}$ -BDC and $\mathrm{c}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC. Simultaneously, through a comparative analysis of the d-band center and catalytic activity between $\mathsf{c}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{N i}_{8}\mathsf{C o}_{2}$ - BDC $(E_{\mathrm{d}}=2.73\ \mathrm{eV}$ , $\eta_{10}=298\mathrm{mV},$ ) and amorphous $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ -BDC $'E_{\mathrm{d}}=2.98\mathrm{eV}$ , $\eta_{10}=274\mathrm{mV}_{.}$ ), it is evident that the amorphous nature also exhibits the ability to alter the position of the d-band center, and thus optimizing the catalytic performance. Based on the above observations, we believe that both the variations in amorphous degree and the $\mathrm{Ni/Co}$ ratio can lead to the modulation of the $E_{\mathrm{d}}$ energy level, consequently influencing the catalytic activity. Nevertheless, the amorphous nature plays a pivotal role, as it can finely tune the $E_{\mathrm{d}}$ energy level, thereby optimizing the electronic structure, and ultimately enhancing the OER performance. The amorphous structure thus emerges as a promising factor in finetuning the catalytic activity and holds great potential for advancing the design of efficient electrocatalysts in OER applications.
